@*
    The Settings page's Appearance tab: pick a theme from ThemeCatalog.Grouped.

    One control, not two. A theme carries exactly one palette and its own ThemeMode, so picking a
    theme picks light or dark with it - there is no separate light/dark control any more, and so no
    way to ask for "Solarized Dark, in light mode" and be shown a different theme's palette instead
    (docs/adr/0017-a-theme-is-a-palette-not-a-pair.md).

    A MudList rather than a MudSelect: MudBlazor 9 has no MudSelectItemGroup, so a MudSelect cannot
    render the Light / Dark / High contrast headings this picker is grouped by. The list also shows
    every theme at once, which is what a theme picker wants - the same shape VS Code's own picker has,
    and the catalog these themes came from.

    The grouping is ThemeCatalog.Grouped's, computed once there rather than here, because
    ThemeCatalog.BuiltIn's ORDER is load-bearing - three call sites read BuiltIn[0] as "the default
    theme" - so the picker must never be tempted to reorder the catalog to group it.

    MudBlazor's MudThemeProvider lives in the render tree (MainLayout.razor), so choosing a theme here
    applies immediately through that component's own parameters - no page reload, no <head> to
    rewrite, no JavaScript interop of this component's own.

    Subscribes to AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged the same way Settings.razor subscribes to
    HookStore.HooksChanged: the store is a singleton, so a hand-edit to appearance.json (or a save
    from another browser tab) must repaint this tab without a restart, and rules.md's "unsubscribe in
    Dispose" reasoning for RoomEvents applies here for the same reason - a leaked subscription to a
    singleton never dies.
*@

@using Agency.Huddle.App.Appearance
@using Agency.Huddle.App.Themes
@implements IDisposable
@inject AppearanceStore AppearanceStore

<p class="teammates-intro">
    Pick a theme. Each theme is either a light or a dark one, so choosing it sets the application's
    light or dark colours too.
</p>

<MudList T="string"
         Class="theme-picker"
         SelectionMode="SelectionMode.SingleSelection"
         SelectedValue="@this.SelectedThemeId"
         SelectedValueChanged="this.OnThemeChangedAsync"
         aria-label="Theme">
    @foreach (var grouping in ThemeCatalog.Grouped)
    {
        <MudListSubheader Class="theme-picker-heading">@grouping.Heading</MudListSubheader>
        @foreach (var theme in grouping.Themes)
        {
            <MudListItem T="string" Value="@theme.Id" Text="@theme.Label" />
        }
    }
</MudList>

<p class="settings-theme-credit">
    Every theme except Huddle Light and Huddle Dark is one of the colour themes
    bundled with Visual Studio Code, mapped onto this application's palette. Visual Studio Code and
    its default themes are Microsoft's, under the MIT licence; Solarized is Ethan Schoonover's and
    Monokai is Wimer Hazenberg's.
</p>

<p class="settings-hooks-file-path">
    The selection is stored at <code>@this.AppearanceStore.FilePath</code>. The file does not exist
    until you save a choice here, so it being absent is expected, not a bug.
</p>

@code {
    /// <summary>The theme id shown selected: the store's current selection, or the catalog's default when nothing is selected yet.</summary>
    private string SelectedThemeId => this.AppearanceStore.Current.ThemeId ?? ThemeCatalog.BuiltIn[0].Id;

    /// <summary>Subscribes to <see cref="AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged"/> - see the file-level comment for why this matters.</summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged += this.OnAppearanceChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>Unsubscribes from <see cref="AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged"/> - see the file-level comment for why this matters.</summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged -= this.OnAppearanceChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Saves the newly picked theme id. A null reaches here when MudList clears its selection, which
    /// SingleSelection does when the selected item is clicked again; the store reads that as "no
    /// theme selected" and the catalog's default applies, which is the right answer either way.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="themeId">The theme id the Human just picked.</param>
    private Task OnThemeChangedAsync(string? themeId)
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.Save(themeId);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private void OnAppearanceChanged()
    {
        _ = this.DispatchAsync(() =>
        {
            this.StateHasChanged();
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });
    }

    // Mirrors Settings.razor's DispatchAsync exactly: a store event can still fire while this
    // component's circuit is winding down, and both exceptions mean there is nothing left to repaint.
    private async Task DispatchAsync(Func<Task> action)
    {
        try
        {
            await this.InvokeAsync(action);
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException)
        {
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
        }
    }
}
